"""Plain HTML fund pages: one document per published fund, data inlined.

**Why not a framework route.** The plan's hosting row chose plain HTML generated by
the pipeline over a route per fund, and the reason is a budget rather than a taste:
a Next route for each of 8,983 funds multiplies files and drags a React payload
onto a page that needs none of it, against a Worker limit of 20,000 files per
version (`static-xray-frontend` §2). One document per fund, no client fetch, no
hydration — the page is complete in its first byte.

**A document of the design system, not a second one.** The page is built from the
same primitives as the app — `.shell .page .title .lede .panel .card .table .bar
.meter .chip .notice .prose .answer .toc`, defined once in `web/app/globals.css` —
and rather than restate the tokens it copies that file byte for byte at build time
and appends the handful of document rules at the bottom of this module. A token
that moves in the app moves here on the next build, so the page cannot drift into
a second palette, and `test_fund_pages.py` proves it by checking that every class
the template uses is defined in the stylesheet it ships. No third-party request,
and no font the app does not already self-host under `/fonts/`.

**The one script, and why it is not load-bearing.** The page ends with a single
inline `<script>` under fifteen lines: it exists so "add this fund" and "compare"
are one code path, and that is all it does. Every action is a real anchor whose
`href` this module computed — `/#v3.…` for the portfolio, `/compare/#v3.…` for the
comparison, `/x/#v3.…` for the ledger — so the page is fully readable and
navigable with JavaScript off, and the script has no `eval`, no `innerHTML`, no
storage, and no request. A generated page cannot be a client component: 9,016 of
them ship as static assets, and a page that only works after hydration would be a
page that does not work.

**One number, one source.** Every value here is read from the published artifact —
the same `funds/{TICKER}.json` the ledger fetches — so a fund page cannot disagree
with the ledger about a weight, a coverage figure, or an `as_of`. Nothing is
recomputed except the presentation of it, and the coverage bands are the contract's
gates (below 60% of weight covered: metrics suppressed, "not covered"; 60–70%:
"partial"), not a second opinion about them. Country tiers are the same idea: the
company→domicile map is the release's own `companies/countries.json`, the tier
table mirrors `web/lib/country-tier.ts` (a test holds the two equal), and a
company the release does not place is drawn as unplaced rather than guessed.

**What the page refuses to do.** It does not round a real position to zero, does not
renormalise as-filed weights to 100%, does not print a number without its `as_of`
and source, and does not hide a fund because its coverage is low — a low-coverage
fund gets a page that says so, in the same place a high-coverage fund says 99.4%.
It never prints a return, a price, an expense ratio, a rating, or a ranking.

**Why rebuild only the funds that changed.** Regenerating all 8,983 pages costs
a few seconds, so the saving is not the generator's time — it is the upload. A
deploy ships `web/out` to the Worker and Cloudflare skips every asset whose bytes
hash to what it already serves, so a daily refresh that rewrites only the funds
whose document changed uploads a dozen files instead of 96 MB. That only holds
while the other 8,973 documents stay byte-identical, which is why `build(only=…)`
writes pages and never deletes, moves, or rewrites anything outside them, and why
a page carries no release-wide field — no release id, no generated-at stamp —
that would make every page different on every day. The three dates a page shows
are the fund's own: period end, filing date, and the lag between them.

**What "changed" means, and what it cannot see.** A fund changed when its
`funds/{TICKER}.json` bytes differ from the parent release, and the bytes are
compared through each release's `manifest.json` per-file `sha256` rather than by
re-hashing 8,983 files: the manifest is the record the validator already
re-checks, so trusting it cannot make this disagree with a release that passed.
One edge sits outside that test — a page also embeds its neighbours, the
same-style bucket's ranked list and the issuer list, both read from
`catalogue.json`, so a fund added, renamed, or re-covered in another fund's row
can change a page whose own document did not move. It embeds the country map too:
a release that only re-decided countries changes every page whose companies moved
tier, and `--only-file` is how that is repaired without a full rebuild's upload.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import base64
import html
import json
import re
import sys
from dataclasses import dataclass
from pathlib import Path

from .artifact import sha256_hex
from .paths import REPO_ROOT
from .style import Style
from .style_overrides import resolve

#: Contract gates (`lookthrough-math-and-qa` §5, conventions I8). Below 60% of
#: weight covered, a concentration figure is not a fact about the fund; it is a
#: fact about our mapping, so it is withheld rather than shown small.
FULL_COVERAGE = 70.0
PARTIAL_COVERAGE = 60.0

#: Rows shown on the page. The full holdings list is the ledger's job; this is the
#: part a reader can actually use, and 25 keeps the document near 14 kB.
TOP_HOLDINGS = 25

#: Same-issuer links, so a reader can move sideways through a family of funds.
SAME_ISSUER_LINKS = 12
SIMILAR_FUND_LINKS = 12

#: A ticker becomes a directory name, so it must be a plain path segment.
SAFE_TICKER = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,12}$")

#: Release-relative paths a page is built from. They are constants rather than
#: literals at each `open()` because `RELEASE_INPUTS` below is the contract another
#: module copies against: a path that exists only inside a function body is a path
#: the mirror cannot know about, which is exactly how `companies/countries.json` came
#: to be missing from mirrored releases while the generator was already reading it.
CATALOGUE_FILE = "catalogue.json"
FUNDS_DIR = "funds"
FUNDS_FILE = f"{FUNDS_DIR}/{{ticker}}.json"
COUNTRIES_FILE = "companies/countries.json"


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class ReleaseInput:
    """One file inside a release directory that a page is built from.

    `path` is relative to the release root; `{ticker}` is expanded once per catalogue
    entry, so `funds/{ticker}.json` is one row of the contract and 9,016 reads.
    `required=False` means a page is still built without the file and says what is
    missing instead of inventing it. `pointer` is where the release pointer publishes
    the object's sha256 — the mirror checks against it when the pointer names this
    release, and `None` means the pointer describes it some other way (the fund
    documents are covered by the per-shard index, not by `release.json`).
    """

    path: str
    required: bool
    what: str
    pointer: tuple[str, ...] | None = None


#: The release contract from the consumer's side: every object a fund page is built
#: from, and whether a page can exist without it. `pipeline.mirror` copies every
#: release-wide entry in this tuple and refuses a copy that is missing a required one,
#: so an input added here cannot silently fail to reach a mirrored release the way the
#: country map did. `test_fund_pages` proves the tuple describes the reads below, and
#: `test_mirror` proves the mirror covers it.
#:
#: `manifest.json` is absent on purpose: it is `--changed-from`'s input, not the page's,
#: and a mirror has no parent release to diff against. `build()` reads it only when the
#: caller asks for an incremental rebuild.
RELEASE_INPUTS: tuple[ReleaseInput, ...] = (
    ReleaseInput(
        CATALOGUE_FILE,
        True,
        "the fund list: names, issuers, ETF flag, coverage",
        pointer=("catalogue",),
    ),
    ReleaseInput(
        FUNDS_FILE,
        True,
        "one document per fund — the page's weights, coverage and metrics",
    ),
    ReleaseInput(
        COUNTRIES_FILE,
        False,
        "the release's company→domicile map; without it the market split says 'Not traced'",
        pointer=("companies", "countries"),
    ),
)


def missing_release_inputs(release_dir: Path, tickers: list[str]) -> dict[str, list[str]]:
    """Which of `RELEASE_INPUTS` a release directory does not hold.

    `tickers` is what the caller set out to publish: a mirror with `--limit` is complete
    for its prefix, not for the whole catalogue, so the caller's list is the measure.
    Returns `{"required": [...], "optional": [...]}` of release-relative paths, with
    `{ticker}` expanded, so a caller can refuse to ship a release missing a required
    input and can report which optional one a page will have to do without.
    """
    missing: dict[str, list[str]] = {"required": [], "optional": []}
    for item in RELEASE_INPUTS:
        paths = (
            [item.path.format(ticker=ticker) for ticker in tickers]
            if "{ticker}" in item.path
            else [item.path]
        )
        gaps = [path for path in paths if not (release_dir / path).is_file()]
        if gaps:
            missing["required" if item.required else "optional"].extend(gaps)
    return missing

#: The app's own fragment grammar (`web/lib/fragment.ts`). A ticker that the ledger
#: would refuse must not be offered as a pre-filled portfolio link.
FRAGMENT_TICKER = re.compile(r"^[A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9.-]{0,9}$")

#: The canonical host. The app is noindexed until the operator promotes a release,
#: and so is this: `--indexable` exists so the decision is one flag, not an edit.
SITE = "https://unpackfund.com"

#: Where the site lives. The sitemap must carry absolute URLs, and a relative one
#: is silently ignored by crawlers, so this is a constant rather than a guess.
SITE_ORIGIN = SITE

#: The app's own routes, in the sitemap's order. Fund pages are added from the
#: catalogue. `/x` is deliberately absent: it is a tool, not a document.
APP_ROUTES = (
    "/",
    "/funds/",
    "/compare/",
    "/c/",
    "/about/",
    "/methodology/",
    "/disclaimer/",
    "/privacy/",
    "/terms/",
)

CSS_NAME = "fund-page.css"

